   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the highway law, in relation to designating a portion of the state highway system as the \"Trooper Christopher G. Skinner Memorial Highway\"",
    "basePrintNo": "S3468",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3468-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §344-d, Hway L",
    "lawSection": "Highway Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S3468\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the highway law, in relation to designating a portion\nof the state highway system as the \"Trooper Christopher G. Skinner\nMemorial Highway\"\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo rename a portion of Interstate Route 81 between exits 6 and 7 in\nthe Town of Chenango the \"Trooper Christopher G. Skinner Memorial\nHighway.\"\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 adds section 342-x to the highway law allowing for the\nexception of a portion of Interstate Route 81 between exits 6 and 7\nfrom \"The American Legion Memorial Highway.\"\n\nSection 2 adds section 344-d to the highway law allowing for the\nnaming of a portion of Interstate Route 81 between exits 6 and 7 to be\nnamed the \"Trooper Christopher G. Skinner Memorial Highway.\"\n\nSection 3 states the Commissioner of Transportation shall provide for\nthe installation and maintenance of adequate signing of the \"Trooper\nChristopher G. Skinner Memorial Highway.\"\n\nSection 4 establishes the act shall take effect immediately\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nOn May 29, 2014, Trooper Christopher G. Skinner was intentionally\nstruck and killed while issuing a traffic ticket on Interstate 81\nbetween Exits 6 and 7 in the Town of Chenango, County of Broome, New\nYork. Trooper Skinner at the time of his death was assigned to the New\nYork State Police Traffic Incident Management (TIM) detail that was\ncreated to ensure the safety of the construction workers and motoring\npublic in work zones on the highways of New York.\n\nThe Work Zone Safety Act of 2005, that created the TIM detail, was\nimplemented following one of the worst work zone accidents in New York\nState history. On May 20, 2005 three highway workers were killed in a\nwork zone on Interstate 81 in the Town of Chenango when a charter bus\nstruck a tractor trailer and started a chain reaction leading to the\ndeaths. Ironically, Trooper Skinner was the first Trooper to arrive on\nthe scene of this horrific accident and became acutely aware of the\nneed for a police presence in the work zones.  When the TIM detail was\nformed, he requested and was assigned to this critical function of the\nhighway safety system.  It is only proper that we forever memorialize\nTrooper Skinner's dedication to service of the people of the State of\nNew York, by naming this portion of Interstate 81 in his honor.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNone.\n\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nEffective Immediately\n\n",
